Our research group has utilized these guidelines to target porous MOAs based on rigid and directional single indiumcentered tetrahedral building units (TBUs) and having nondefault topologies.[5] As MOAs from p-block metals are rare and our previous studies with indium involve rigid building blocks based on the single-metal ions and organic chelating moieties with N,O-donors, we began to explore conditions to synthesize other potential indium-based building blocks, specifically those derived from metal carboxylates. Metal– carboxylate clusters often generate a rigid node with fixed geometry involving multiple metal–oxygen coordination bonds which induce the stability of the node and subsequently enhance the thermal stability and overall rigidity of the framework.[1d–f,6] To date, most contributions have focused primarily on the use of transition-metal-based clusters, such as, but not limited to, dimeric MBBs $\left([\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathbf{CO}_{2})_{4}]\right.$ square paddlewheels[7]) and tetrameric MBBs $\left([\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}]\right.$ clusters[8]).
Initial experiments with indium and linear ditopic carboxylate-based organic linkers, not surprisingly, led to the assembly of single-metal-ion-based TBUs $\left(\left[\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{4}\right]\right)$ into metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) having diamond-like topology, the default arrangement for tetrahedral nodes.[9] However, under similar reaction conditions, the use of angular organic linkers, such as 1,3-benzenedicarboxylic acid $(120^{\circ})$ , consistently led to the in situ formation of a novel oxygen-centered indium–carboxylate trimer molecular building block (TMBB), $\left[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}\right]$ .
Note that oxo-bridged trimers with the formula $[\bf{M}_{3}\mathrm{O}-\$ $(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{R})_{6}\mathbf{L}_{3}]^{n+}$ are common in transition-metal coordination chemistry and are structurally well-established with over 389 structures in the Cambridge Structure Database (CSD; August, 2006).[10] However, 3D MOF structures based on the assembly of TMBBs are still scarce.[11] Only recently have F?rey and co-workers reported routes to synthesize porous metal–carboxylate structures from TMBBs, where the metal– acetate trimer acts as a precursor.[12]
Our approach is to extend this type of TMBB to contain pblock elements, such as indium. Although serendipity lead to the formation of this unprecedented cluster, a new avenue has been opened to derive and generate novel extended structures based on the $\left[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{6}\right]$ TMBB.[13] Herein, we report the synthesis and structure of two MOFs based on the novel oxo-bridged trinuclear indium–carboxylate clusters, one having a cubic structure similar to $\mathbf{MIL-}59^{[11\mathbf{d}]}$ with a $\mathrm{CaB}_{6}$ topology and the other exhibiting an unprecedented soc topology ( $\mathbf{soc}=$ square–octahedron).
Reaction between 1,3-benzenedicarboxylic acid (1,3- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC)}$ and $\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a $N,N$ -dimethylformamide $\mathrm{(DMF)/CH_{3}C N}$ solution in the presence of imidazole yields a homogeneous microcrystalline material.[14] The as-synthesized compound was characterized and formulated by elemental microanalysis and single-crystal $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray diffraction studies as $[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(C_{8}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{1.5}(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{3})(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{0.5}]\cdot$ $\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}0.5(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})$ (1).[15] The purity of 1 was confirmed by similarities between its simulated and experimental powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD). The cubic structure of 1 (Figure 1) is built from trimers of corner-sharing octahedrally coordinated indium centers joined by the bent 1,3-BDC organic linkers. The trimers contain three indium-centered octahedra that share one central $\upmu_{3}–\mathbf{O}\mathbf{X}\mathbf{O}$ anion located on a threefold axis, which leads to three In- $(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O})$ -In angles of $120^{\circ}$ . Crystallographic analysis shows that the apical positions of the indium ions in the trimer building unit are statistically occupied by water molecules and by deprotonated and neutral imidazole molecules. Each trimer unit is linked by six separate ditopic organic linkers to build up the 3D structure. The framework exhibits similar cuboidal cages to those in MIL-59, and is isostructural to MIL-59 (Figure 1).
Similar reaction conditions as for 1 in the presence of piperazine and $3{,}3{^\prime}{,}5{,}5{^\prime}$ -azobenzenetetracarboxylic acid[16,17] as the organic linker give orange polyhedral crystals formulated as $[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(C_{16}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{6})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}](\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})$ (2) by elemental microanalysis and single-crystal $\mathbf{X}$ -ray diffraction studies.[18] The crystallographic analysis of 2 revealed that its structure contains similar indium trimer building blocks. The trimers contain three $\{\mathrm{InO}_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\}$ octahedra sharing one central $\upmu_{3}$ -oxo anion. In each octahedron, the apical position is occupied by a terminal water molecule. Each trimer unit is linked by six separate organic linkers to produce a novel 3D structure. To better understand the framework topology, the components of 2 can be simplified as 4-connected rectangular planar nodes (organic linker) and 6-connected nodes (indium trimeric building units), as seen in Figure 2 a,b. The assembly of these two types of nodes results in the generation of a 3D network having the soc topology[19,20] (Figure 2d). In the crystal structure of 2 (Figure 2 c), each indium atom is trivalent, yielding an overall cationic framework $(+1$ per formula unit) that is balanced by $[\mathrm{NO}_{3}]^{-}$ ions. The disordered $[\mathrm{NO}_{3}]^{-}$ ions occupy statistically two positions on the threefold axis with equal probability. Therefore, a total of four $[\mathrm{NO}_{3}]^{-}$ ions reside in each nanometer-scale carcerand-like capsule of 2, and are not able to escape owing to steric hindrance (window dimensions: 7.65 $1\stackrel{\triangledown}{\vec{\mathbf{A}}}\times5.946\stackrel{\triangledown}{\vec{\mathbf{A}}}$ , point to point and not including van der Waals radii). Other interesting structural features of 2 are its two types of infinite channels. The first type is hydrophilic, because the water molecules coordinated to the indium centers are pointed inside these channels. Guest water molecules occupy the remaining free volume in these channels and form hydrogen bonds with coordinated water molecules. The second type of channels, with approximately 1-nm diameter, is guest-free in the as-synthesized 2.[21]
The total solvent-accessible volumes for 1 and 2 were determined by summing voxels more than $1.2\mathring\mathrm{A}$ away from the framework using PLATON software. They were estimated to be $18.9\%$ (the coordinated imidazolate ligands were not omitted in this calculation) and $57.2\%$ , respectively. The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption study for 2 revealed a reversible type I isotherm with no hysteresis, characteristic of a microporous material with homogeneous pores (Figure 3 a). The estimated Langmuir surface area and pore volume for 2 are $1417\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.50\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively.
Owing to the interesting structural and functional features of 2, that is, the narrow pores with higher and localized charge density, we explored the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake on 2 and found that it can store high amounts, up to $2.61\%$ at $78\mathrm{K}$ and $1.2\mathrm{atm}$ (Figure 3b). The isosteric heat of adsorption, up to $1.8\%$ loading of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per sorbent weight, was calculated to be $q=$ $6.5\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ (Figure 3 c), a value appreciably larger than that observed for carbon porous materials[22] and similar to the values for MOFs.[23] The constancy of the heat of adsorption for uptake up to $1.8\%$ per weight, the maximum amount sorbed at $87\mathrm{K}$ and 1.2 atm, is indicative of the homogeneity of the sorption sites for 2 up to this experimental loading (ca. $70\%$ of the full loading of $2.61\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $78\mathrm{K}$ and $1.2\mathrm{atm}\rangle$ ). This result reveals the potential influence of open metal sites, framework charges, and pore dimensions on the energetics of sorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules in MOFs. The importance of open metal sites on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ sorption has, in fact, been confirmed for several MOFs,[24] as well as the impact of electrostatic interactions of the sorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ within the pores of charged frameworks (zeolites).[25] The large amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed on 2 $(2.61\%)$ suggest a higher density of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ within the pores $(0.05\mathrm{gcm}^{-3})$ when compared to values for other MOFs.[3a]
This higher population of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules in the pores approaches a liquid-like state $(0.0708\ \mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ at $20\mathrm{K}$ ) at atmospheric pressures and higher temperatures $(78\mathrm{K})$ , probably driven by the narrow pores $(1\mathrm{nm})$ that result in an overlap of potential energy fields of the pore walls combined with the residential charge density within the pores.
To obtain a better understanding of the sorption sites within 2, inelastic neutron scattering (INS) was used to study the respective interactions of molecular $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with the framework. The INS spectra of the rotational transitions of the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules were obtained at $15\mathrm{K}$ on 2 by using in situ loading of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at doses equivalent to one, two, three, five, and seven $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per indium atom. The spectra shown in Figure 4 are of a similar quality to those obtained for hydrogen in several other MOFs,[24] and thereby indicate the presence of reasonably well-defined binding sites. As in previous studies, we find that even at the lowest loading of $1\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ , multiple sites are occupied, despite the fact that the interaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at the vacant indium coordination sites (created by removal of the apical water molecules upon dehydration) ought to be the strongest.[24] This finding is in accord with the relatively constant value of isosteric heats of adsorption at loadings below $1.8\%$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake (Figure 3 c).
To assign the considerable number of observed peaks, it is necessary to use a model for the rotational potential experienced by the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule, detailed in the Supporting Information. At the $1\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ loading, several peaks are observed, which validates the concurrent occupation of multiple sites at this relatively low uptake. The three most intense bands may be assigned as 0–1 and 0–2 transitions for weakly bound $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as listed in the Supporting Information, Table S1. According to our model, the well-defined peak at about $25.5\mathrm{meV}$ is likely to be associated with the vacant indium site. The fact that the assignment of the rotational transitions for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at this site can be made based on our model suggests that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is physisorbed, not coordinated, to the exposed metal site, which is in contrast to previous observations.[26] We can make inferences about the nature of the weak binding sites from the dependence of the INS spectra on $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loading. The principal observed effect of extra loading up to 3 $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ is an increased intensity of the strong bands around 12.8 and $14{\mathrm{meV}};$ , that is, the filling of more of the non-indium sites, which supports our assignments and indicates predominant occupation of most of the vacant indium sites at 1 $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ . Since we have identified the open-indium sites, the identity of the remaining sites can be associated with the organic components of the framework, that is, carboxylate, azo, and phenyl moieties. Note that at loadings higher than 5 $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ $\mathrm{\tilde{H}}_{2}$ uptake greater than $1.8\%$ and close to the maximum $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake of $2.61\%$ at $78\mathrm{K}$ and $1.2\mathrm{atm}\dot{}$ ) some additional binding sites become available (Supporting Information, Table S1) with stronger interactions (lower energies compared to the sites occupied by $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at reduced loadings of 1 and $3\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}\ '$ ). This situation, which is apparent in the difference spectra (Figure 4b), is in marked contrast to previous observations in MOFs, where sites with weak guest–host interactions are typically populated at the highest loadings.[23]
This unique INS finding at the higher loading of $5\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ potentially, can be correlated to the unique structural features of 2, namely the presence of isolated nanometer-scale carcerand-like capsules, which anchor nitrate ions, and which are strictly accessible through the two main channels by very restricted windows (Supporting Information, Figure S4). Accordingly, we believe that the isolated cages containing nitrate ions are not accessible at lower loadings owing to the higher sorption kinetic-energy diffusion barrier associated with the restricted entrance. This barrier is pronounced at the low experimental temperature of $78\mathrm{K}$ We believe that at $78\mathrm{K}$ and at lower loadings below $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}}/\ensuremath{\mathrm{In}}$ , primarily the channels are occupied, and that at higher loadings, close to $5\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{In}$ , the isolated cages become accessible owing to increased local concentration (pressure) of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ near the cavity entrances, which allows the limiting sorption kinetic energy diffusion barrier to be overcome. Accordingly, access to such narrow cavities which enclose high local charge density permits the exposure of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules to stronger sorption sites, as supported by the INS studies at higher loadings. These observations are completely in accord with the elevated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ density $(0.05\mathrm{gcm}^{-3})$ observed in the pores of 2 at 78 K.[27]
The structural analysis of 2, combined with the INS and sorption studies, suggests that narrower pores $(<1\mathrm{nm})$ and/or higher localized charge densities can be suitable for higher uptake of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Therefore, we believe that MOFs possessing these characteristics, combined with the modularity of their construction that allows higher surface areas to be obtained, will permit the attainment of the US Department of Energy (DOE) target for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake in the near future.
Currently, we are exploring several avenues to increase the uptake, including the ability to design and synthesize porous MOFs combining high surface area, higher charge densities, and narrow pores around $1\mathrm{nm}$ , similar to those obtained in 2. Also, the ability to generate trimeric clusters from several lighter metals offers great potential to synthesize an $\left[\mathrm{Al}_{3}\mathrm{O(CO}_{2})_{6}\right]$ trimer based soc MOF, which will theoretically adsorb similar amounts of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per unit volume and, most importantly, higher uptake per weight unit (estimated to be $3.0\%$ ).
